WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
Welcome to Module 3, PostgreSQL in Action.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:09.407
In Module 2, we focused on how a relational database is organized.

00:00:09.407 --> 00:00:14.277
Now we start using that database by reading rows, filtering results, and making controlled changes.

00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:20.837
The main goal is not to memorize a long list of SQL commands.

00:00:20.837 --> 00:00:24.847
Instead, I want you to develop a safe working pattern: understand the question,

00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:31.282
write the smallest useful statement, run it, inspect the result, and verify any change before keeping it.

00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:38.572
Many slides include PostgreSQL code that runs directly in your browser.

00:00:38.572 --> 00:00:44.012
You can test the examples here first, then use Copy and paste the same SQL into the pgAdmin

00:00:44.012 --> 00:00:46.957
Query Tool for more practice.

00:00:46.957 --> 00:00:52.622
We will begin with SELECT and filtering, then move carefully into INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, transactions,

00:00:52.622 --> 00:00:59.427
and JSONB. Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:59.427 --> 00:01:03.342
an analyst exploring operational records before building a report.

00:01:03.342 --> 00:01:10.577
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:10.577 --> 00:01:14.112
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:20.877
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:20.877 --> 00:01:24.517
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:28.649
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
